.TH DUVIS 1 "2026-05-24"
.SH NAME
duvis \- ASCII visualization of du disk usage information
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B duvis
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-0 ]
.RB [ \-\-unsorted ]
.RB [ \-\-print0 ]
.RB [ \-\-font\-size " " \fIpt\fR ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The
.I duvis
program provides an ASCII visualization of the output of
.IR du (1)
suitable for understanding how storage is being used. The
output of
.I duvis
is the paths that were input, with
only the last component shown except at the root, indented
according to nesting depth, and sorted at each level by
decreasing size, with ties broken alphabetically.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \-p
Ingest preorder-laid-out input: lex-sort the entries
before building the tree. Use this when the input was not
produced by an in-order traversal of
.IR du (1)
(for example, when it has been sorted or filtered).
.IP \-g
Open a GTK4/Cairo graphical view in the style of
.IR xdu (1).
The view is a recursive rectangle split: each node gets a
header band labeling it, and its children are laid out
proportionally in the area below, alternating horizontal
and vertical splits with the aspect ratio.
.IP \-r
Raw output: emit entries in the order they are currently
held in the internal array, indented by depth. Useful for
debugging ingestion.
.IP \-0
Treat input records as NUL-terminated rather than
newline-terminated. Pair with
.BR "du \-0" .
.IP \-\-unsorted
Do not sort children by size at display time; preserve the
order in which the tree was built.
.IP \-\-print0
NUL-terminate output records instead of using newlines (cf.
.BR "find \-print0" ).
.IP "\-\-font\-size \fIpt\fR"
Font size in points for the GUI label text. Defaults to
.BR 10 .
Affects the height of each node's header band as well.
.SH USAGE
.PP
As with
.IR xdu (1),
you invoke
.I duvis
on the output of
.IR du (1).
If a
.I file
argument is given, input is read from there; otherwise it
is read from standard input, so either a pipe or a file is
fine. The
.I du
output must be complete, in the sense that every prefix of
every path in the file has an entry (with the exception of
the common prefix that was given to
.IR du );
both relative and absolute paths work.
